{
  "_doc": "How each building is built in 3D (scripts/world3d/building_model.gd). Sizes are in tiles. body is the main block: w and d as shares of the footprint, h its wall height, at [x, z] its centre as shares of the footprint (0.5, 0.5 is the middle); walls: boards, logs, stone, plaster or metal. roof: gable (ridge along the longer side), hip, shed, flat or dome; pitch is rise over half-span. wall colour defaults to the catalog item's colour. features add the details that tell buildings apart: yard (a fenced dirt or straw yard on the rest of the footprint), silo, stilts, ramp, chimney, cupola, loft_door, stall_doors, porch, terrace, nest_boxes, water_trough, hay_bales, beehives, trees, pond, sign. levels: what each bigger size changes, keyed by size (\"2\", \"3\"...), laid on in turn: body, roof and door values replace the smaller size's, windows and window_rows replace, features are added, and drop removes features of the named types first. Upgrade features: wing (an annex with its own roof: side right or front, depth, span, h), lean_to (an open shelter on posts), weathervane. Under construction, a building is built in stages as materials arrive (building_model.gd build_site). fill: what shows how full the building is (scripts/world3d/building_fill.gd): produce piles up at 'at' (eggs, milk, wool, truffles, honey, hay, crates) as collection nears; trees: how many of the trees it holds stand in it (an orchard). Animals inside wander out in the yard, more as it fills.",
